WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.020
Disrupting the CAS4 gene in mice impairs microglia containment of amyloid plaques and accelerates

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:13.120
Alzheimer's-like pathology. This is Planetarian Daily, episode 252. Essential gene mutation

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.580
rates, a sugar hydrogel that gains strength under strain, and machine vision checked against

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:24.480
brain recordings follow, with a week in review. Researchers at UVA Health in findings carried

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.980
by science daily showed that the cast-4 gene regulates microglia, the brain's immune

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.180
cells that surround and compact amyloid beta plaques.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.480
In laboratory mice, those plaques expanded and spread more aggressively once that regulation

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.660
failed, and neurons sitting next to the deposits took increased damage.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.860
Cognitive function in the animals declined, and the genes effects appeared concentrated

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:49.660
in immune cells rather than neurons.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:55.480
bonds. Defective Cas4 activity may serve as an early biomarker, and boosting its function

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.220
could offer a treatment approach. More than six million Americans live with Alzheimer's,

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.960
the six leading cause of death in the United States. Losing Cas4 also triggered changes in

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.780
the activity of other genes, with potential implications for additional neurodegenerative

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:17.091
diseases. Mutations scored across bacteria and yeast genomes showed no sign that essential

00:01:17.091 --> 00:01:22.471
genes change less often than other genes. Earlier work that reported lower mutation rates

00:01:22.471 --> 00:01:27.251
in critical genes likely missed the harmful variants because natural selection removed

00:01:27.251 --> 00:01:32.731
them before anyone could detect them. The result challenges a 50-year-old assumption that evolution

00:01:32.731 --> 00:01:38.866
protects important genes from change at the mutation level itself. A team at Washington University

00:01:38.866 --> 00:01:45.286
in St. Louis, in work described by Fizz.org, built a biodegradable hydrogel from sugar crystals

00:01:45.286 --> 00:01:49.746
molecules, and polyethylene glycol that becomes stronger when it is bent or stretched.

00:01:50.366 --> 00:01:55.466
Cycle dextrin rings in the gel slide into position under load, and reinforce the regions

00:01:55.466 --> 00:02:00.066
taking the strain, which leaves the polymer chains more uniform and more organized.

00:02:00.646 --> 00:02:05.666
The material was tested on a flexible knee-like joint and with stood 10,000 bending cycles

00:02:05.666 --> 00:02:07.106
while performing as predicted.

00:02:07.606 --> 00:02:11.446
It is compatible with three-dimensional printing and with biological tissues.

00:02:11.446 --> 00:02:16.986
news. Suggested uses include supporting artificial joints or repairing damaged tissues in places

00:02:16.986 --> 00:02:22.426
where strength is needed, only where the load actually falls. The polymer mimics collagen

00:02:22.426 --> 00:02:28.380
in the human body. Artificial neural networks that model vision predicted brain cell responses

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:35.420
to 1,320 natural and synthetic images, in tests at York University reported by Science Daily.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:40.540
Yet brain activity poorly predicted many of the model's internal features when the comparison

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.580
and ran in reverse. That mismatch did not appear when the same kind of comparison was made

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:50.680
between neuronal activity recorded in different brains. The pattern indicates that the machines

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:56.300
can match brain outputs while using different internal strategies. The team also ran 300

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:01.860
further images presented as outlines, drawings, and artistic styles, and those extra sets

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:07.799
confirmed the divergence. K-RAS mutations, immune cell contacts, and tissue changes that

00:03:07.799 --> 00:03:13.519
happened before a pancreatic tumor can be seen are being charted in work carried by BGNES.

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:18.439
The mapping covers early cellular alterations and how those cells interact with immune

00:03:18.439 --> 00:03:20.119
cells and with surrounding tissue.

00:03:20.859 --> 00:03:24.099
Most of those early tissue changes do not progress to malignancy.

00:03:24.699 --> 00:03:29.879
The work focuses on how inflammation and failed immune clearance may let abnormal cells persist.

00:03:31.075 --> 00:03:36.415
Monash University researchers argue in an account from science daily that species such as

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:40.635
Australopithecus and paranthropus should be grouped inside the genus Homo.

00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:45.795
Additional fossils and evolutionary analyses show that the traditional boundaries between

00:03:45.795 --> 00:03:48.595
these ancient relatives have become difficult to maintain.

00:03:49.435 --> 00:03:54.455
Haitian cave fossils collected in 1984 and long labeled as a woodcock were sequenced

00:03:54.455 --> 00:04:00.475
by Florida museum scientists, in a study noted by Fizz.org, and identified as a snipe.

00:04:01.095 --> 00:04:05.615
Woodcocks and snipes diverged from a common ancestor about 25 million years ago.

00:04:05.615 --> 00:04:11.835
The same project pulled DNA from museum specimens of 29 living species and from one additional

00:04:11.835 --> 00:04:15.235
extinct snipe to build a time-calibrated phylogeny.

00:04:15.895 --> 00:04:19.795
Eight living woodcock species remain and six of them are endemic to islands.

00:04:20.876 --> 00:04:26.296
Researchers in a study summarized by Science Daily hold more divided views on what constitutes

00:04:26.296 --> 00:04:28.436
science than members of the general public.

00:04:28.896 --> 00:04:33.136
That variation may help explain public frustration with scientific uncertainty.

00:04:33.136 --> 00:04:39.596
In brief, Yan Yin found in work noted by science news that scientists who revised rejected

00:04:39.596 --> 00:04:44.676
proposals using feedback reached success faster than those who switched to entirely new ideas.

00:04:45.356 --> 00:04:51.476
Sebnom etcha-exe, after identifying memory-linked proteins in prostate cancer cells, is mapping

00:04:51.476 --> 00:04:56.156
nerve fibers inside tumors to learn how cancer cells communicate with the nervous system.

00:04:57.067 --> 00:05:02.227
Most people assume the most important genes are somehow shielded from mutation, as if evolutionary

00:05:02.227 --> 00:05:06.867
important itself lowered the error rate, and that is something most people get wrong.

00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:12.287
The actual pattern arises from natural selection acting after a mutation has already occurred,

00:05:12.707 --> 00:05:16.367
not from a quieter mutation process in the genes that matter most.

00:05:16.967 --> 00:05:21.387
Harmful changes in essential genes are more likely to block reproduction or survival,

00:05:21.867 --> 00:05:27.047
so those damaged variants vanish from populations before anyone can count them in living organisms.

00:05:27.047 --> 00:05:37.807
Surveys that look only at surviving genomes therefore record lower mutation counts in critical genes even though the original chemical process of mutation shows no such bias.

00:05:38.467 --> 00:05:44.667
Once that filtering by selection is accounted for mutation frequencies look equivalent across gene categories.

00:05:45.227 --> 00:05:51.607
The distinction changes how geneticists read variation data when they estimate disease risk or evolutionary timelines.

00:05:51.607 --> 00:05:57.647
efforts to predict mutation hotspots should focus on biochemical features of DNA rather

00:05:57.647 --> 00:06:00.007
than on how important a gene is thought to be.

00:06:00.427 --> 00:06:04.907
Across the past seven days, methods for reading living systems without tearing them apart

00:06:04.907 --> 00:06:06.047
kept landing here.

00:06:06.507 --> 00:06:12.047
CRISPR edits scored cell by cell, whale genomes taken from breath, and a hypothalamus atlas

00:06:12.047 --> 00:06:13.567
built from hundreds of scans.

00:06:14.147 --> 00:06:19.607
A single cell sequencing method mapped intended CRISPR edits and the unintended ones, and tracked

00:06:19.607 --> 00:06:21.587
how those edits change gene activity.

00:06:22.307 --> 00:06:27.087
Drones collected whale blow and recovered complete genomes without a biopsy or any need

00:06:27.087 --> 00:06:28.167
to disturb the animals.

00:06:28.827 --> 00:06:33.587
An atlas of the human hypothalamus traced how volume and gene expression shift with aging

00:06:33.587 --> 00:06:39.287
and Alzheimer's across 458 imaging scans and 614,000 nuclei.

00:06:39.967 --> 00:06:45.227
Before we go, whole genome sequencing of early embryonic cells, taken before selection

00:06:45.227 --> 00:06:49.247
has acted, would settle remaining questions about raw mutation rates.

